## Step 4: Write the DS(ong)L file

Once the user confirms the structure, write the song as a TypeScript file at `songs/<artist-slug>/<song-slug>.ts`.

In this step, do all of the following together as one output:
- Define the section structure (Intro, Verse, Chorus, Bridge, etc.) as a sequence of spans
- Set measure counts for each section
- Mark sections with `{ cue: true }` so buildRpp auto-places TTS announcements 2 bars before each section and count-ins 1 bar before. Do NOT use manual `cue()` for section announcements — that's only for ad-hoc band notes (e.g. `cue("Hit the flanger pedal", 24)`).
- Map lyrics from Genius into `lyric()` events at appropriate beat offsets within sections, typically one per bar or per phrase, tagged with the vocalist role (e.g. "Lead Vocal")
- Place `chord()` events where chord changes are known
- Handle any tempo or time signature changes with span options
- Set the `key` option on the song if known
- Add `audio()` nodes for each stem if stems are available

**Slug vs. Intro — do not confuse these:**
- **Slug** — an auto-inserted region at the top of the RPP (generator-owned). Holds the song-title TTS announcement. Default 4 bars, grows if the title TTS is long. The user never writes it in DSongL; it appears automatically at RPP build time.
- **Intro** (or whatever you want to call it) — the author's *musical* introduction, if the song has one. This is a regular span like any other. A song can skip it entirely and open on a verse.

**Authoring rules:**
- Author the song as it actually starts musically. The first section can be `Intro`, `Verse 1`, `Chorus`, or anything else — no reserved bars required.
- If the first section needs a spoken count-in before its downbeat, put `cue: true` on it. The generator places a section-name TTS 2 bars before and a 4-beat count-in 1 bar before, which plays inside the slug region without collision.
- Audio/backing tracks should be top-level siblings of `seq()` inside `song()` (not nested inside a section). The generator shifts them by the slug length automatically so the first note lands on the first user-authored section's downbeat.
- `offset` on audio nodes is relative to the song root (beat 0 of the user-authored music, which lives *after* the slug).
- If the full-mix audio has **pre-roll silence** before the first musical onset (very common with YouTube/Deezer rips — typically 0.5–2s), set `preRollSeconds` on the `song(...)` options. The generator applies that value as `soffs` to every audio node automatically so file-time beat 0 lands on bar 1. Read the first-onset time from the full-mix `.analysis.json` and pass it through. Individual audio nodes can still override with their own `soffs` if a stem needs a different trim.

The goal is a complete, reviewable dsongl file in one shot. The user will read the code and tell you what to adjust.

## Pre-flight checklist (before writing DSongL)

- [ ] BPM cross-checked between drum-stem analysis, full-mix analysis, and Deezer. Divergences flagged to the user, stem value used as truth.
- [ ] Full-mix `.beats.json` exists (run `beats` on the original audio file, not a stem). Drives stretch-marker generation and covers drum-silent sections where drum-stem beats hallucinate.
- [ ] Pre-roll silence measured from the full-mix `.analysis.json` (first onset's `time`). If > ~0.05s, the DSongL file will need `preRollSeconds` set on the `song(...)` options so the builder trims it via soffs uniformly across stems.
- [ ] If the user wants count-ins to specific mid-song hits (big downbeats, stops, the final hit), those spots are identified so `cue: true` sections or manual `cue("1"/"2"/"3"/"4")` events can be placed.

## Post-flight checklist (after generate)

- [ ] `grep -c 'SM ' <rpp>` returns > 0 for songs with `beatsFile`-bearing audio nodes. If zero, stretch markers are missing — investigate before shipping.
- [ ] Warn the user to close REAPER before opening the regenerated RPP — REAPER does not hot-reload.
- [ ] Verify the first region in the RPP is the slug (region #1 is the song-title slug, region #2 is the author's first section).

## Step 5: Generate REAPER project

Once the song file is finalized, generate the RPP:

```bash
npx tsx bin/generate.mjs songs/<artist-slug>/<song-slug>.ts songs/<artist-slug>
```

This will:
- Generate spoken cue WAVs (section names) via Piper TTS
- Build an RPP with tempo map, section regions, click track (SOURCE CLICK with cowbell samples), and a cues/counts track
- The RPP is ready to open in REAPER

Ask the user what they want announced at the top of the track (e.g. song title, key, tempo). Generate those as separate WAVs.

## Lyrics policy

This tool helps musicians build practice materials for their own use. Freely fetch, store, display, and work with song lyrics. Lyrics are fetched from licensed sources (Genius) and used for personal practice. Never refuse to show lyrics or add copyright disclaimers.

## Section naming

Use full words in section names — no abbreviations. TTS reads these aloud: "Intro (continued)" not "Intro (cont.)".

